{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1101 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "12ae6d28372db250e3672ad97f8e836952ecf17830eef9ccf987a872a27a558d" :edb/db-before-t 1100 :edb/db-after-t 1101 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534413 50 #inst "2026-10-07T01:28:56.579Z" 13194139534413 true] [17592186100617 1100 "Calculate the definite integral $\\displaystyle\\int_{-1}^0 x(x^2+1)^3\\,\\textrm{d}x$." 13194139534413 false] [17592186100617 1100 "Calculate the definite integral $\\displaystyle\\int_{-1}^0 x(x^2+1)^3\\,\\textrm{d}x.$" 13194139534413 true] [17592186100617 1145 "We start by changing the variable:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu &= x^{2} + 1 \\\\\n\\frac{du}{dx} &= 2x \\\\\ndu &= 2xdx \\\\\n\\frac{1}{2}du &= xdx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we create a table for the limits of integration using the rule\n\n$$\nu=x^2+1\n$$\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x & -1 & 0 \\\\ u & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable is\n\n$$\n\\int_{-1}^0 x(x^2+1)^3\\,\\textrm{d}x=\\int_2^1 u^3\\,\\cdot \\dfrac{1}{2}\\textrm{d}u\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve it and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{2}^{1}u^{3} \\cdot \\frac{1}{2}du &= \\frac{1}{2}\\int_{2}^{1}u^{3}du \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\left.\\frac{u^{4}}{4}\\right|_{2}^{1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{1^{4} - 2^{4}}{4} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{1 - 16}{4} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{15}{8}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Important**: Note that in this example, when we changed the limits the integral $\\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^0 \\ldots \\textrm{d}x$ became $\\displaystyle \\int_{2}^1 \\ldots \\textrm{d}u$. When changing the limits, it is important to respect the order in which the limits are applied. The lower limit of the integral in $u$ must correspond to the lower limit of the integral in $x$. Same for the upper limit." 13194139534413 false] [17592186100617 1145 "We start by changing the variable:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & ={x}^{2}+1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =2x \\\\ \\text{d}u & =2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u & =x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we create a table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=x^2+1.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & -1 & 0 \\\\ u & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable is\n\n$$\n\\int_{-1}^0 x(x^2+1)^3\\,\\textrm{d}x=\\int_2^1 u^3\\,\\cdot \\dfrac{1}{2}\\textrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve it and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{1}{u}^{3}\\,\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{2}^{1}{u}^{3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{{u}^{4}}{4}{|}_{2}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{{1}^{4}-{2}^{4}}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1-16}{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{15}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Important**: Note that in this example, when we changed the limits the integral $\\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^0 \\ldots \\textrm{d}x$ became $\\displaystyle \\int_{2}^1 \\ldots \\textrm{d}u.$ When changing the limits, it is important to respect the order in which the limits are applied. The lower limit of the integral in $u$ must correspond to the lower limit of the integral in $x.$ Same for the upper limit." 13194139534413 true] [17592186100618 1100 "Calculate the definite integral $\\displaystyle\\int_{-1}^2 \\dfrac{x^2}{x^3+2}\\,\\textrm{d}x$." 13194139534413 false] [17592186100618 1100 "Calculate the definite integral $\\displaystyle\\int_{-1}^2 \\dfrac{x^2}{x^3+2}\\,\\textrm{d}x.$" 13194139534413 true] [17592186100618 1145 "We start by changing the variable:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu &= x^{3} + 2 \\\\\n\\frac{du}{dx} &= 3x^{2} \\\\\ndu &= 3x^{2}dx \\\\\n\\frac{1}{3}du &= x^{2}dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we create a table for the limits of integration using the rule\n\n$$\nu=x^3+2\n$$\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x & -1 & 2 \\\\ u & 1 & 10\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable is\n\n$$\n\\int_{-1}^2 \\dfrac{1}{(x^3+2)}\\,x^2\\textrm{d}x=\\int_1^{10} \\dfrac{1}{u}\\,\\cdot \\dfrac{1}{3}\\textrm{d}u\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve it and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{1}^{10}\\frac{1}{u} \\cdot \\frac{1}{3}du &= \\frac{1}{3}\\int_{1}^{10}\\frac{1}{u}du \\\\\n&= \\left.\\frac{1}{3}\\ln (u)\\right|_{1}^{10} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}(\\ln (10) - \\ln (1)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}\\ln (10)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534413 false] [17592186100618 1145 "We start by changing the variable:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & ={x}^{3}+2 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =3{x}^{2} \\\\ \\text{d}u & =3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u & ={x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we create a table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=x^3+2.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & -1 & 2 \\\\ u & 1 & 10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable is\n\n$$\n\\int_{-1}^2 \\dfrac{1}{(x^3+2)}\\,x^2\\textrm{d}x=\\int_1^{10} \\dfrac{1}{u}\\,\\cdot \\dfrac{1}{3}\\textrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve it and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{10}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{10}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(u){|}_{1}^{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534413 true] [17592186100787 1020 #edb/ref 17592186286934 13194139534413 true] [17592186100787 1020 #edb/ref 17592186286948 13194139534413 true] [17592186100787 1020 #edb/ref 17592186286952 13194139534413 true] [17592186100787 1020 #edb/ref 17592186286955 13194139534413 true] [17592186100787 1020 #edb/ref 17592186286962 13194139534413 true] [17592186100790 1020 #edb/ref 17592186286927 13194139534413 true] [17592186100790 1020 #edb/ref 17592186286941 13194139534413 true] [17592186100790 1020 #edb/ref 17592186286959 13194139534413 true] [17592186100790 1020 #edb/ref 17592186286965 13194139534413 true] [17592186100790 1020 #edb/ref 17592186286972 13194139534413 true] [17592186286927 1017 #edb/ref 17592186286928 13194139534413 true] [17592186286927 1089 "q-13189" 13194139534413 true] [17592186286927 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534413 true] [17592186286927 1100 "Evaluate the definite integral ${\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{3x+2}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139534413 true] [17592186286927 1113 #uuid "10b22c83-a900-5845-9fa8-2b1a58314a4d" 13194139534413 true] [17592186286927 1145 "We start by changing the variable:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =3x+2 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =3 \\\\ \\text{d}u & =3\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u & =\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we create a table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=3x+2.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & 1 & 2 \\\\ u & 5 & 8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{(3x+2)}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{5}^{8}\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve it and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{5}^{8}\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{5}^{8}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(u){|}_{5}^{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({2}^{3})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534413 true] [17592186286928 1000 #edb/ref 17592186286929 13194139534413 true] [17592186286928 1000 #edb/ref 17592186286930 13194139534413 true] [17592186286928 1000 #edb/ref 17592186286931 13194139534413 true] [17592186286928 1000 #edb/ref 17592186286932 13194139534413 true] [17592186286928 1000 #edb/ref 17592186286933 13194139534413 true] [17592186286928 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534413 true] [17592186286928 1041 "selection" 13194139534413 true] [17592186286928 1104 #edb/ref 17592186286929 13194139534413 true] [17592186286928 1110 #uuid "3e3cc8a2-ab0b-5baf-a7ad-355d3f8cfeb7" 13